WHY IT IS VENDORED AND NOT FETCHED, measured before the act (OPS-070(B)):
the governance repo that owns this gate has ZERO git remotes (`git remote -v`
returns nothing, measured 2026-09-20). A GitHub Actions runner cannot fetch a
file from a repository that exists on one laptop and nowhere else. "CI reaches
across the seam" is not a design choice here, it is mechanically impossible.
Copying it in is the only way `d18-2:b` can be executed at all.

THE COST, NAMED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER. This is now a FORK. Two copies of
HIE's instrument exist and CI CANNOT DETECT DRIFT BETWEEN THEM, for exactly the
reason above: the runner cannot see the other copy. If HIE improves the gate,
this copy silently keeps the old behaviour and every run stays green while
testing a stale instrument. That is a success-shaped value, and this unit has a
memory of one.

WHAT CARRIES THE DRIFT INSTEAD, and its honest bound: `tool/glance_gate.sh
--drift-check` compares this file's md5 with the governance copy. It runs ONLY
on a machine holding both trees -- i.e. never in CI. It is a local check a seat
runs, not a loom that fires on its own. ⛑ UNVERIFIED, never cleared: nothing
schedules it. Stated here so the next reader does not mistake the vendored copy
for a governed one.

RE-VENDORING: change the governance copy, then re-copy and update the md5 above.
